.TH HOC 1
.SH NAME
hoc \- interactive desk-calculator language, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hoc
[
.I file
|
.BI \-e " expr"
| \- ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hoc
is a structural Limbo translation of hoc6, Kernighan and Pike's desk-
calculator language from
.IR "The Unix Programming Environment" ,
plan9port's
.B src/cmd/hoc
(no 9front source tree could be found on this host to diff against -
see BUGS). It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
Each command-line argument is processed in order: a bare
.B \-
or the absence of any arguments reads from standard input;
.BI \-e " expr"
(attached or as a separate argument) runs
.I expr
as a one-line program; anything else is a file name. Errors abandon
the rest of the CURRENT argument and move on to the next one, the same
way the original's own
.BR setjmp / longjmp
recovery does (see BUGS) - hoc always exits 0 regardless.
.SS Language
A program is a sequence of statements and function/procedure
definitions, one top-level construct parsed and (if not a definition)
executed immediately before the next is even looked at - this matters
for
.BR read() ,
below. Expressions use the usual arithmetic
.RB ( + - * / % ^ ),
comparison
.RB ( == "" != "" < "" <= "" > "" >= ),
logical
.RB ( && "" || ! ),
assignment
.RB ( = "" += "" -= "" *= "" /= "" %= ),
and increment/decrement
.RB ( ++ / -- ,
both prefix and postfix). A bare top-level expression prints its own
value; a bare top-level assignment does not.
.PP
.B print
.I expr-or-string , ...
prints each item separated by nothing but a single trailing space
after each NUMERIC item - there is no added newline at all, so a
script wanting one ends its list with an explicit
.B "\e"n\e""
string.
.B _
(underscore) is a real variable holding the last value a bare
top-level expression printed.
.PP
.BI read( var )
reads one number directly off the CURRENT input stream (the same
stream the program's own text is coming from) into
.I var ,
evaluating to 1 on success or 0 at real end of input. Since a whole
top-level construct - including a
.B while
loop's entire body - is fully parsed before any of it executes, the
classic idiom
.B "while (read(x)) ..."
reads data values that appear AFTER the loop's own closing brace in
the same file, not interleaved with it.
.PP
.BR func " and " proc
define a function (must return a value) or procedure (must not) taking
by-value parameters; recursion works by temporarily rebinding the same
shared global for the duration of one call, restored on return.
.B return
with no expression is only valid inside a
.B proc
(and vice versa for
.BR "return expr" ),
but this is a RUNTIME check the first time that particular
.B return
actually executes, not a parse-time one - a
.B func
containing an untaken
.B return
with no value never trips it.
.PP
Built in:
.BR sin ", " cos ", " tan ", " atan ", " asin ", " acos ", " sinh ", "
.BR cosh ", " tanh ", " log ", " log10 ", " exp ", " sqrt ", " int ", "
.BR abs ", "
and the constants
.BR PI ", " E ", " GAMMA ", " DEG ", " PHI .
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/hoc.b
(translated from plan9port's
.BR src/cmd/hoc ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoctest (1)
.SH BUGS
No 9front copy of hoc's source could be located on this host to diff
against plan9port's (the usual first check for this backlog) - see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
and
.B hoc.b 's
own header comment. hoc6 is a well-documented, widely archived
program, so the risk of an unnoticed divergence is believed low, but
unlike this backlog's other files, it is honestly unverified rather
than diffed clean.
.PP
Two of
.BR && / || 's
operands are ALWAYS both evaluated - they do not short-circuit, unlike
most other languages including this tree's own bc(1)/dc(1) - confirmed
directly, not assumed:
.B "print (0 && (1/0))"
still reports a division by zero.
.PP
Binary
.B %
is a real
.BR fmod() ;
.B %=
is not the same operation - it truncates BOTH operands to an integer
FIRST, then does integer modulo
.RI ( "7.5 % 2"
is 1.5, but
.RI ( "x=7.5; x %= 2" )
gives 1) - a real asymmetry in the original, preserved rather than
"fixed" for consistency. That truncation uses Limbo's 32-bit
.BR int ,
not the host's own 64-bit C
.BR long ,
an unreproduced platform detail for very large operands.
.PP
Every printed number uses
.B "%.17g"
verbatim, matching the source - but the real oracle's own
.B print()
(Plan9's libfmt) appears to print the shortest decimal that still
round-trips to the same value, capped at 17 significant digits, not
always exactly 17 the way this port's (and glibc's)
.B %.17g
does:
.B "print 0.1"
shows
.B 0.1
on the real binary but
.B 0.10000000000000001
here. Characterized, not fixed - matching it would need a full
.B strtod()
this tree doesn't have, just to chase a formatting-library difference
below either implementation's own code.
.PP
No recursion-depth cap: the original stops a runaway recursive call
at a clean 100-frame limit; this port recurses natively through
Limbo's own interpreter call stack instead, so it fails some other way
past that point rather than with that specific message.
.PP
No fixed-length identifier/string caps (the original's own 100-byte C
buffer, with a "name too long"/"string too long" error) - Limbo
strings aren't a fixed buffer, so there's nothing to hit. A final
top-level construct with no trailing newline before EOF is accepted
here (the real oracle reports a syntax error there instead) - a small,
deliberate leniency, not a faithfully-reproduced edge case.
